WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.259 --> 00:00:01.907
- You're putting yourself in front

00:00:01.907 --> 00:00:03.898
of so many people just by going like,

00:00:03.898 --> 00:00:04.958
"I'll give it a shot."

00:00:04.958 --> 00:00:06.376
And yeah, it might not work.

00:00:06.376 --> 00:00:07.835
And yeah, and when it doesn't work,

00:00:07.835 --> 00:00:08.899
some people might be like,

00:00:08.899 --> 00:00:10.127
"You, you tried."

00:00:10.127 --> 00:00:11.756
Well, them, try again.

00:00:11.756 --> 00:00:12.908
Some people get blocked.

00:00:12.908 --> 00:00:14.119
Everyone gets a really good idea of like,

00:00:14.119 --> 00:00:15.015
"I want to do something."

00:00:15.015 --> 00:00:16.916
Sometimes you sit around, watch
a movie, listen to podcasts,

00:00:16.916 --> 00:00:18.356
you're like, "I want to
do that," then they stop,

00:00:18.356 --> 00:00:20.127
'cause like, "I dunno how,
I don't have the money."

00:00:20.127 --> 00:00:22.576
Money is a hurdle that's
kind of easy to get over.

00:00:22.576 --> 00:00:23.766
To just hear how simple it is.

00:00:23.766 --> 00:00:25.674
Sometimes you just need
somebody to be like,

00:00:25.674 --> 00:00:28.007
"It's this simple, dude,"
and give you three steps.

00:00:28.007 --> 00:00:29.667
and suddenly you're like, "That's it?

00:00:29.667 --> 00:00:30.936
"I can take it the rest of the way."

00:00:30.936 --> 00:00:32.805
Getting started is
always the hardest part.

00:00:32.805 --> 00:00:35.376
If you're here listening to
me talk about this stuff,

00:00:35.376 --> 00:00:38.153
you are not a film fan,
you're a film maker.

00:00:38.153 --> 00:00:39.734
You just haven't made a film yet.

00:00:39.734 --> 00:00:41.506
Go out and make your film.

00:00:41.506 --> 00:00:42.766
- He's an American film maker,

00:00:42.766 --> 00:00:45.457
actor, comedian and podcaster.

00:00:45.457 --> 00:00:48.489
He rose to prominence for his
low-budget comedy film Clerks,

00:00:48.489 --> 00:00:51.746
in which he acted, directed
and co-produced it.

00:00:51.746 --> 00:00:54.688
He's also known for his
long, humorous Q&amp;A sessions

00:00:54.688 --> 00:00:58.197
which are often filmed and
distributed for DVD release.

00:00:58.197 --> 00:00:59.869
He's Kevin Smith, and here's my take

00:00:59.869 --> 00:01:01.927
on his top 10 rules of success.

00:01:01.927 --> 00:01:03.549
Rule number one is my personal favorite.

00:01:03.549 --> 00:01:04.769
And make sure to stick around all the way

00:01:04.769 --> 00:01:06.707
to the end for some special bonus clips.

00:01:06.707 --> 00:01:08.518
Also, as Kevin is talking,
if he says something

00:01:08.518 --> 00:01:10.635
that really resonates
with you, please leave it

00:01:10.635 --> 00:01:12.788
in the comments below
and put quotes around it

00:01:12.788 --> 00:01:14.889
so other people can be inspired as well.

00:01:14.889 --> 00:01:15.722
Enjoy.

00:01:18.677 --> 00:01:21.427
(dramatic music)

00:01:28.705 --> 00:01:31.167
- You can do what's expected, absolutely,

00:01:31.167 --> 00:01:35.018
and sometimes you'll have the
expected or hoped for result.

00:01:35.018 --> 00:01:37.368
But there's so many doors into this thing,

00:01:37.368 --> 00:01:38.669
and I'm not just talking about film,

00:01:38.669 --> 00:01:41.447
I'm talking about everything
that you're interested in life.

00:01:41.447 --> 00:01:43.049
There are so many doors,

00:01:43.049 --> 00:01:45.409
and we all look right at
the front door and go,

00:01:45.409 --> 00:01:47.409
"How do we get through,
there's a massive line?"

00:01:47.409 --> 00:01:48.599
Go to the back door.

00:01:48.599 --> 00:01:50.349
Go to the side window.

00:01:50.349 --> 00:01:52.157
Dynamite a hole in the wall.

00:01:52.157 --> 00:01:53.388
You belong in the room too,

00:01:53.388 --> 00:01:56.209
you don't have to wait to
be invited into something.

00:01:56.209 --> 00:01:57.538
You know, I'm here to tell you,

00:01:57.538 --> 00:02:00.768
nobody will ever invite
you into something amazing

00:02:00.768 --> 00:02:02.618
'cause they're like, "You, it's ours."

00:02:02.618 --> 00:02:04.298
So if you want to be involved in something

00:02:04.298 --> 00:02:06.527
and you want to see
something that doesn't exist,

00:02:06.527 --> 00:02:08.287
all you have to do is try.

00:02:08.287 --> 00:02:10.687
You're putting yourself
in front of so many people

00:02:10.687 --> 00:02:12.977
just by going like, "I'll give it a shot."

00:02:12.977 --> 00:02:14.289
And yeah, it might not work.

00:02:14.289 --> 00:02:16.717
And yeah, when it doesn't work,
some people might be like,

00:02:16.717 --> 00:02:18.086
"You, you tried."

00:02:18.086 --> 00:02:19.718
Well, them, try again.

00:02:19.718 --> 00:02:20.551
That's it, man.

00:02:20.551 --> 00:02:22.125
That's all we're here to do is try.

00:02:22.125 --> 00:02:24.923
Because at the end of this,
there's no winner, we all die.

00:02:24.923 --> 00:02:26.087
So just try.

00:02:26.087 --> 00:02:29.277
Try, try every day before
they stick you in the grave.

00:02:29.277 --> 00:02:30.687
And I'm not talking
about moving mountains,

00:02:30.687 --> 00:02:33.007
I'm talking about all that
you sit there and go like,

00:02:33.007 --> 00:02:34.127
"I'm going to do that one day."

00:02:34.127 --> 00:02:34.960
Do it tomorrow.

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:35.967
Do it right now.

00:02:35.967 --> 00:02:37.526
Just give it a shot.

00:02:37.526 --> 00:02:40.057
Because maybe it takes a
day, maybe it takes a week,

00:02:40.057 --> 00:02:42.834
maybe it takes five years, but
you don't get there without

00:02:42.834 --> 00:02:44.324
just giving it that first step.

00:02:44.324 --> 00:02:45.807
And I get it, the first step's hard,

00:02:45.807 --> 00:02:47.626
they say the journey of a million miles

00:02:47.626 --> 00:02:49.467
begins with a single step.

00:02:49.467 --> 00:02:51.298
What they don't say is,
single step is harder

00:02:51.298 --> 00:02:53.695
than every other mile
that comes after that.

00:02:53.695 --> 00:02:56.444
All those miles are easy
once you take that first one,

00:02:56.444 --> 00:02:58.925
but I understand how difficult
it is to take the first step.

00:02:58.925 --> 00:03:00.868
Look at me, I'm a fat
man, and I read every day,

00:03:00.868 --> 00:03:02.966
stop being fat, you're going to die.

00:03:02.966 --> 00:03:04.188
And I'm all like, I will, one day.

00:03:04.188 --> 00:03:05.805
And I never take that first step, man,

00:03:05.805 --> 00:03:07.386
and so I continue to be fat.

00:03:07.386 --> 00:03:10.543
But in everything else in life,
I can take that first step.

00:03:10.543 --> 00:03:12.303
So, I know what I'm talking about, kids.

00:03:12.303 --> 00:03:14.487
Like, try, just give it a shot, man.

00:03:14.487 --> 00:03:17.967
Don't worry what say, and I'll
clear the mystery for you,

00:03:17.967 --> 00:03:20.187
will say everything, dude.

00:03:20.187 --> 00:03:22.354
You can build a cathedral,

00:03:23.304 --> 00:03:26.322
and your steeple will break the clouds,

00:03:26.322 --> 00:03:28.402
and someone will down below be yelling,

00:03:28.402 --> 00:03:29.947
"You're doing it wrong!"

00:03:29.947 --> 00:03:31.461
Don't listen to those people.

00:03:31.461 --> 00:03:34.151
Life moves forward, man, and
people that want to tell you

00:03:34.151 --> 00:03:36.081
you suck are usually behind you.

00:03:36.081 --> 00:03:39.404
So, keep moving forward
and try, try everyday.

00:03:39.404 --> 00:03:41.272
My first piece of advice, don't
ask anybody for money, man.

00:03:41.272 --> 00:03:42.363
I'm looking at the wrong place.

00:03:42.363 --> 00:03:43.455
They'll never going to give me the job

00:03:43.455 --> 00:03:44.288
if I look at the monitor.

00:03:44.288 --> 00:03:45.321
- [Woman] You can look at
me, that's good enough.

00:03:45.321 --> 00:03:46.201
- But I was playing to her.

00:03:46.201 --> 00:03:47.461
I was trying to make a connection.

00:03:47.461 --> 00:03:48.393
HuffPost Live, as they say.

00:03:48.393 --> 00:03:49.270
- [Woman] Alright, so you've done it.

00:03:49.270 --> 00:03:50.103
Camera one, go for it.

00:03:50.103 --> 00:03:52.070
- Don't go to people for money, man.

00:03:52.070 --> 00:03:53.469
You don't need that much money,

00:03:53.469 --> 00:03:55.330
unless you're making some
kind of underwater epic,

00:03:55.330 --> 00:03:56.630
you don't need to go to somebody.

00:03:56.630 --> 00:03:57.939
If there are no more gatekeepers,

00:03:57.939 --> 00:03:59.687
I mean there are, if you want
their money, by all means,

00:03:59.687 --> 00:04:01.361
but then you got to take
their notes and stuff.

00:04:01.361 --> 00:04:03.349
But you don't have to wait
for somebody to tell you,

00:04:03.349 --> 00:04:04.729
"Yes, you may proceed."

00:04:04.729 --> 00:04:05.837
You don't have to go out there

00:04:05.837 --> 00:04:07.471
with your hand held out and whatnot.

00:04:07.471 --> 00:04:09.590
Of course, there's like
Indiegogo, Kickstarter,

00:04:09.590 --> 00:04:11.049
you can raise a budget that way

00:04:11.049 --> 00:04:12.901
and you're essentially
not begging for money,

00:04:12.901 --> 00:04:15.699
you're pre-selling all your
DVDs and whatnot in advance

00:04:15.699 --> 00:04:17.310
to help you finance your dream.

00:04:17.310 --> 00:04:20.686
But don't get that thinking,
kind of incorrect thinking

00:04:20.686 --> 00:04:21.839
out of your head, man.

00:04:21.839 --> 00:04:24.278
You don't have to wait or ask somebody

00:04:24.278 --> 00:04:26.278
or approach a financier.

00:04:27.175 --> 00:04:28.521
I did it on credit cards.

00:04:28.521 --> 00:04:30.829
Bunch of credit cards that
I'd kind of put together.

00:04:30.829 --> 00:04:31.688
I always feel like...

00:04:31.688 --> 00:04:32.948
- Suze Orman is dying somewhere.

00:04:32.948 --> 00:04:33.888
- Somewhere, yeah, right?

00:04:33.888 --> 00:04:34.721
She's sitting there going,

00:04:34.721 --> 00:04:36.371
"I said I was starting my first film."

00:04:36.371 --> 00:04:37.204
It's weird, she goes,

00:04:37.204 --> 00:04:38.660
"I'm starting my first
film, but I need money."

00:04:38.660 --> 00:04:40.485
I'm like, "Well, then you're
not starting your first film,"

00:04:40.485 --> 00:04:41.945
or you can start your first film

00:04:41.945 --> 00:04:43.745
without needing that money at all.

00:04:43.745 --> 00:04:44.832
Some people get blocked.

00:04:44.832 --> 00:04:46.231
Everyone gets like a
really good idea of like,

00:04:46.231 --> 00:04:47.064
"I want to do something."

00:04:47.064 --> 00:04:48.832
Sometimes you sit around, watch
a movie, listen to podcast,

00:04:48.832 --> 00:04:49.840
you're like, I want to do that.

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:50.850
Then they stop 'cause they're like,

00:04:50.850 --> 00:04:52.229
"I dunno how, I don't have the money."

00:04:52.229 --> 00:04:54.428
The money is a hurdle that's
kind of easy to get over,

00:04:54.428 --> 00:04:55.759
particularly in the age of technology

00:04:55.759 --> 00:04:57.309
where this magical box right here,

00:04:57.309 --> 00:04:58.711
or the ones we have in our pockets,

00:04:58.711 --> 00:05:00.648
will shoot a movie, you can edit a movie,

00:05:00.648 --> 00:05:03.259
you can upload it to YouTube,
all from the same device,

00:05:03.259 --> 00:05:05.159
all within like an hour
or something like that.

00:05:05.159 --> 00:05:06.951
So, when that technology's at your hands,

00:05:06.951 --> 00:05:08.620
sometimes people are a
little afraid about like,

00:05:08.620 --> 00:05:10.150
well, I don't know how to use it or,

00:05:10.150 --> 00:05:12.898
they say that making movies takes money,

00:05:12.898 --> 00:05:15.368
and of course, it does
take a little bit of lucre,

00:05:15.368 --> 00:05:16.468
but not as much as like,

00:05:16.468 --> 00:05:19.150
that should stop somebody from going out

00:05:19.150 --> 00:05:21.809
to finance their own dream or
go out looking for finance.

00:05:21.809 --> 00:05:22.978
But that's the thing I try to curb,

00:05:22.978 --> 00:05:24.528
don't ask people for money.

00:05:24.528 --> 00:05:27.428
If you do that, then you'd
just give yourself an excuse

00:05:27.428 --> 00:05:29.018
to never really take the next step

00:05:29.018 --> 00:05:30.870
'cause you're like, well,
I couldn't find the budget.

00:05:30.870 --> 00:05:32.408
Like, a lot of people
ask me, they're like,

00:05:32.408 --> 00:05:34.675
"I was where you were
once, can't you help me?"

00:05:34.675 --> 00:05:35.807
And I was like, "Well, the difference was,

00:05:35.807 --> 00:05:38.237
"when I was where you were,
never asked anybody for help.

00:05:38.237 --> 00:05:39.578
"I just did it myself."

00:05:39.578 --> 00:05:41.516
So, it's always better if
you can do it yourself.

00:05:41.516 --> 00:05:43.098
You feel better at the end of the day.

00:05:43.098 --> 00:05:45.089
And then you're not bugging
anybody with your hand out.

00:05:45.089 --> 00:05:46.091
You don't owe anybody.

00:05:46.091 --> 00:05:47.339
Nobody's got anything over ya.

00:05:47.339 --> 00:05:48.619
And they're also not telling ya, like,

00:05:48.619 --> 00:05:49.452
"Hey, you know that vision

00:05:49.452 --> 00:05:51.182
"that you've been carrying
around your whole life?

00:05:51.182 --> 00:05:53.335
"Change it because there's
something about it that bugs me

00:05:53.335 --> 00:05:54.693
"and since I'm shelling out the dough,

00:05:54.693 --> 00:05:56.353
"you got to do that sort of thing."

00:05:56.353 --> 00:05:57.874
First time out, especially, don't be,

00:05:57.874 --> 00:05:59.241
God, my hair looks terrible.

00:05:59.241 --> 00:06:00.993
First time out (laughs) especially,

00:06:00.993 --> 00:06:03.444
don't go looking for money
from somebody else, man.

00:06:03.444 --> 00:06:04.762
Especially that first film of yours,

00:06:04.762 --> 00:06:07.754
because you're trying
to get you out there.

00:06:07.754 --> 00:06:09.608
The most you you'll
ever have a chance to do

00:06:09.608 --> 00:06:11.290
is when you're making
your first flick, man.

00:06:11.290 --> 00:06:15.801
If you've got a movie fan,
you've got a movie maker.

00:06:15.801 --> 00:06:18.280
Like, I've never met a film maker

00:06:18.280 --> 00:06:21.052
that wasn't a movie buff first.

00:06:21.052 --> 00:06:21.959
I don't care who it is.

00:06:21.959 --> 00:06:22.983
Whether it's like, me,

00:06:22.983 --> 00:06:26.731
or great directors like
Scorsese and Quentin Tarantino,

00:06:26.731 --> 00:06:29.751
there are hardcore movie
fans who still imbibe.

00:06:29.751 --> 00:06:32.960
So, I don't think it's
possible to be a film maker

00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:35.211
unless you're a film fan.

00:06:35.211 --> 00:06:36.343
So, I always encourage everyone

00:06:36.343 --> 00:06:37.983
in those Q&amp;A's where I'm like,

00:06:37.983 --> 00:06:40.621
"If you're here listening
to me talk about this stuff,

00:06:40.621 --> 00:06:43.430
"you are not a film fan,
you're a film maker,

00:06:43.430 --> 00:06:45.070
"you just haven't made a film yet."

00:06:45.070 --> 00:06:46.199
Go out and make your film,

00:06:46.199 --> 00:06:47.871
because who knows better
how to do this stuff

00:06:47.871 --> 00:06:51.032
than the people that consume
it on a regular basis?

00:06:51.032 --> 00:06:52.031
And, you know, it's fine, like,

00:06:52.031 --> 00:06:54.750
the Internet is a distraction
that allows people

00:06:54.750 --> 00:06:58.132
to express themselves
about movies, you know,

00:06:58.132 --> 00:07:01.059
and you can be funny and crappy
about, like crap on movies

00:07:01.059 --> 00:07:03.960
and stuff, but it pulls
you away from like,

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:07.060
there was no Internet when I
started, so I made a movie.

00:07:07.060 --> 00:07:08.041
If the Internet existed,

00:07:08.041 --> 00:07:10.371
I probably would've commented
on other people's movies.

00:07:10.371 --> 00:07:11.921
But because it didn't exist, I was like,

00:07:11.921 --> 00:07:14.011
"Well, let me try to make
one of these movies myself."

00:07:14.011 --> 00:07:16.398
And now I live in a world
where people comment on it,

00:07:16.398 --> 00:07:18.701
largely I get very wonderful things said,

00:07:18.701 --> 00:07:19.922
and then I get a bunch of people

00:07:19.922 --> 00:07:21.542
saying crappy things as well.

00:07:21.542 --> 00:07:26.331
But none of that exits without
me doing something first.

00:07:26.331 --> 00:07:27.638
Without me making something.

00:07:27.638 --> 00:07:29.141
So, I always like to
tell that audience, like,

00:07:29.141 --> 00:07:31.242
"We're not different, I was you."

00:07:31.242 --> 00:07:32.931
I sat there watching other people's stuff

00:07:32.931 --> 00:07:35.500
and I just got to a place
where I was like, why them?

00:07:35.500 --> 00:07:36.533
Why not me?

00:07:36.533 --> 00:07:37.366
Like, if this counts as a movie,

00:07:37.366 --> 00:07:39.233
why can't I make a movie and stuff?

00:07:39.233 --> 00:07:40.066
Which is funny 'cause that's

00:07:40.066 --> 00:07:41.292
what my movies make people do now.

00:07:41.292 --> 00:07:43.330
They're like, "If Yoga Hosers
counts, I'm making a movie."

00:07:43.330 --> 00:07:44.163
(laughter)

00:07:44.163 --> 00:07:45.463
And that's a good call to arms.

00:07:45.463 --> 00:07:46.839
I'm not even insulted by that.

00:07:46.839 --> 00:07:49.143
I'm like, good, whatever
makes you step forward

00:07:49.143 --> 00:07:50.630
and try your self-expression.

00:07:50.630 --> 00:07:53.363
'Cause the only reason anyone
knows my name, or my work,

00:07:53.363 --> 00:07:54.951
and I've been doing this for 22 years,

00:07:54.951 --> 00:07:56.552
I haven't had to get a real job,

00:07:56.552 --> 00:07:58.643
is because it started with
a first step of going like,

00:07:58.643 --> 00:08:00.082
"Why can't I do that?"

00:08:00.082 --> 00:08:02.512
You know, I see people do
it and that's interesting

00:08:02.512 --> 00:08:05.133
and I like their stories but
never saw my world represented

00:08:05.133 --> 00:08:08.012
up on the screen and that's
where I looked for myself.

00:08:08.012 --> 00:08:09.920
I never found myself in a classroom,

00:08:09.920 --> 00:08:11.552
I never found myself in a church, man.

00:08:11.552 --> 00:08:14.626
Closest moments I've ever
had to finding out who I am,

00:08:14.626 --> 00:08:15.742
have been in the art.

00:08:15.742 --> 00:08:17.641
Sitting in a movie theater,
watching a movie, man.

00:08:17.641 --> 00:08:18.474
It's the dark mirror.

00:08:18.474 --> 00:08:20.062
You see yourself reflected in a weird way.

00:08:20.062 --> 00:08:22.512
In reading and listening
to music, watching TV,

00:08:22.512 --> 00:08:24.381
these were the closest
moments where I was like,

00:08:24.381 --> 00:08:26.798
"Oh, I think this is who I
am," and stuff like that.

00:08:26.798 --> 00:08:29.048
So that's within everybody.

00:08:30.030 --> 00:08:34.729
I've spent 21 years of my
career chasing a better career,

00:08:34.729 --> 00:08:36.810
chasing somebody else's career,

00:08:36.810 --> 00:08:38.528
because that's all you
have to go by, right?

00:08:38.528 --> 00:08:40.241
You look at those who've gone before you.

00:08:40.241 --> 00:08:44.324
So my whole career, I've
always aimed for a mark,

00:08:45.419 --> 00:08:48.580
and constantly missed it, never hit it.

00:08:48.580 --> 00:08:52.158
So by my own standards, very unsuccessful.

00:08:52.158 --> 00:08:55.110
But in the process of
doing that for 21 years,

00:08:55.110 --> 00:08:56.971
which I believe is three score.

00:08:56.971 --> 00:08:59.560
One more score and I'm Gettysburg.

00:08:59.560 --> 00:09:02.727
I believe the idea is, over that time,

00:09:05.568 --> 00:09:08.485
I established what it is that I do.

00:09:10.073 --> 00:09:13.303
In my quest to be somebody
else like John Hughes,

00:09:13.303 --> 00:09:15.431
to have the career of a John Hughes,

00:09:15.431 --> 00:09:17.690
I wound up having the
career of a Kevin Smith,

00:09:17.690 --> 00:09:19.671
which to me is like, eh, whatever.

00:09:19.671 --> 00:09:21.561
But, oddly enough, there
are a bunch of people

00:09:21.561 --> 00:09:23.349
that are like, "I'll kill
to have your career."

00:09:23.349 --> 00:09:25.390
And that's really weird and wonderful.

00:09:25.390 --> 00:09:26.954
So any time you get a chance to sit down

00:09:26.954 --> 00:09:29.279
and talk to those cats, you
just want to remind them,

00:09:29.279 --> 00:09:31.069
"Hey, you can have this career, man."

00:09:31.069 --> 00:09:33.551
I backed into and locked
into in a lot of places,

00:09:33.551 --> 00:09:34.921
but most of it is just persistence

00:09:34.921 --> 00:09:37.211
and willingness to try stupid.

00:09:37.211 --> 00:09:39.074
A lot of people don't want to try

00:09:39.074 --> 00:09:41.652
things that might not work,
you know what I'm saying?

00:09:41.652 --> 00:09:43.260
Everyone wants to to try the sure thing,

00:09:43.260 --> 00:09:45.529
and there is no such
thing as the sure thing.

00:09:45.529 --> 00:09:47.068
And if you're looking
at somebody else's work

00:09:47.068 --> 00:09:49.610
to try to gage your own,
as I have for years,

00:09:49.610 --> 00:09:52.109
you will constantly feel like a failure.

00:09:52.109 --> 00:09:55.610
Instead, concentrate
on telling your story.

00:09:55.610 --> 00:09:57.566
This is the thing I'm real big on.

00:09:57.566 --> 00:10:00.282
Your voice is your only
currency in this life,

00:10:00.282 --> 00:10:01.574
that's all you got.

00:10:01.574 --> 00:10:04.802
Things you see and perceive,
the prism through which

00:10:04.802 --> 00:10:07.553
you process all the
information that you take in.

00:10:07.553 --> 00:10:10.001
I'd say you're the most unique
supercomputer on the planet,

00:10:10.001 --> 00:10:11.282
unique from everybody else.

00:10:11.282 --> 00:10:14.792
That perspective is
valuable, insanely valuable,

00:10:14.792 --> 00:10:16.502
'cause nobody else can give it but you.

00:10:16.502 --> 00:10:18.713
And that's why it's always
better to shoot weird

00:10:18.713 --> 00:10:20.542
or shoot different or shoot original

00:10:20.542 --> 00:10:22.742
as opposed to shoot what works.

00:10:22.742 --> 00:10:26.332
Or go for a successful model
or, as I've tried to do,

00:10:26.332 --> 00:10:29.121
try to be John Hughes
or something like that.

00:10:29.121 --> 00:10:31.455
You'll never succeed that way.

00:10:31.455 --> 00:10:34.015
If you try using your own
voice, you try expressing

00:10:34.015 --> 00:10:36.027
your own thoughts that
nobody else could do,

00:10:36.027 --> 00:10:38.177
that's your best shot at success,

00:10:38.177 --> 00:10:40.047
because that's the thing
that you bring to table

00:10:40.047 --> 00:10:41.617
that nobody else could bring.

00:10:41.617 --> 00:10:43.384
And if your timing is fantastic,

00:10:43.384 --> 00:10:45.117
you say the right thing at the right time,

00:10:45.117 --> 00:10:46.665
you could have a career.

00:10:46.665 --> 00:10:47.877
So when you get into this business,

00:10:47.877 --> 00:10:49.706
they tend to tell you to shape it up,

00:10:49.706 --> 00:10:51.485
take off the edges and
make it look like this,

00:10:51.485 --> 00:10:53.505
"Hey, that worked, do that."

00:10:53.505 --> 00:10:57.895
But all we shoot for that
thing that you want to do.

00:10:57.895 --> 00:11:00.054
I remember all of that, and I
remember all the good, though.

00:11:00.054 --> 00:11:01.075
That's the important thing.

00:11:01.075 --> 00:11:02.765
You can't shake the bad, it's like,

00:11:02.765 --> 00:11:03.854
you talk to a hockey player,

00:11:03.854 --> 00:11:05.668
they'll tell you every goal they missed,

00:11:05.668 --> 00:11:06.997
but they can't remember the ones that they

00:11:06.997 --> 00:11:08.168
(coughing drowns out speaker).

00:11:08.168 --> 00:11:10.390
You'll remember the that you miss.

00:11:10.390 --> 00:11:12.599
And, you know, the
negative sticks with us.

00:11:12.599 --> 00:11:14.041
Negative floats right into the surface,

00:11:14.041 --> 00:11:17.202
because it kind of allows us
to open a dialog with ourselves

00:11:17.202 --> 00:11:20.081
about incredulity, or
maybe I'm not good enough.

00:11:20.081 --> 00:11:24.522
It allows you to wallow in
self-esteem issues and whatnot.

00:11:24.522 --> 00:11:26.011
We rarely hear the good.

00:11:26.011 --> 00:11:28.011
We can't hear the good, we're
not programmed for that.

00:11:28.011 --> 00:11:29.552
And I can't tell you how many times people

00:11:29.552 --> 00:11:31.571
have said wonderful things to me in print,

00:11:31.571 --> 00:11:33.642
in real life, and I couldn't hear it

00:11:33.642 --> 00:11:35.401
'cause I'm just listening
to this asshole over here

00:11:35.401 --> 00:11:37.276
telling me I'm terrible at my job.

00:11:37.276 --> 00:11:38.969
(laughter)

00:11:38.969 --> 00:11:41.444
It sticks with you and you
have to train yourself,

00:11:41.444 --> 00:11:44.286
and maybe if you're smart
from the job, you realize,

00:11:44.286 --> 00:11:46.741
before you even start down the path,

00:11:46.741 --> 00:11:48.657
that it doesn't matter what anybody says,

00:11:48.657 --> 00:11:50.163
the game's always on your stick.

00:11:50.163 --> 00:11:53.373
I'm sorry, it was very tried
and tired hockey metaphor.

00:11:53.373 --> 00:11:54.273
(laughter)

00:11:54.273 --> 00:11:55.271
That's where I am in my life.

00:11:55.271 --> 00:11:56.604
The game is always on your stick

00:11:56.604 --> 00:11:59.712
and nobody can take it off
and tell you differently.

00:11:59.712 --> 00:12:00.882
There's a dude in the
stands who'll tell you

00:12:00.882 --> 00:12:02.161
you're doing it wrong.

00:12:02.161 --> 00:12:05.462
They will always tell you that
because critics are people

00:12:05.462 --> 00:12:06.791
that know where they're going,

00:12:06.791 --> 00:12:08.771
they just don't know how to drive a car.

00:12:08.771 --> 00:12:10.781
And so of course they'll
tell you how to drive the car

00:12:10.781 --> 00:12:13.940
every step of the way,
but don't listen to that.

00:12:13.940 --> 00:12:16.332
And I'm not saying don't
listen to critics (mumbles),

00:12:16.332 --> 00:12:19.172
I'm just saying don't listen
to anybody who says you don't,

00:12:19.172 --> 00:12:22.142
or anybody that's like, the
negative's going to be there.

00:12:22.142 --> 00:12:24.482
Try to lend an ear to the positive.

00:12:24.482 --> 00:12:26.792
And that's something that
I've really had to train

00:12:26.792 --> 00:12:28.710
and try to do over my lifetime.

00:12:28.710 --> 00:12:30.722
I don't know why, I'm just predisposed

00:12:30.722 --> 00:12:32.722
to hear and believe negative.

00:12:32.722 --> 00:12:35.341
And it's a nice thing
when (audio cuts out)

00:12:35.341 --> 00:12:38.923
step up and say beautiful
things about the work you do,

00:12:38.923 --> 00:12:42.224
and that's what you got to remember.

00:12:42.224 --> 00:12:44.198
Being in a room with this many people

00:12:44.198 --> 00:12:47.648
who sit there and groove
on it and kind of,

00:12:47.648 --> 00:12:49.627
they want to be there.

00:12:49.627 --> 00:12:52.179
We showed this movie
on Sundance first time,

00:12:52.179 --> 00:12:54.109
and it was nice, but wasn't like this,

00:12:54.109 --> 00:12:55.889
'cause on Sundance, you've
got a lot of interest

00:12:55.889 --> 00:12:57.642
and you sit there like this.

00:12:57.642 --> 00:12:59.981
(laughter)

00:12:59.981 --> 00:13:00.852
- [Woman] Amuse me.

00:13:00.852 --> 00:13:01.685
- Exactly.

00:13:01.685 --> 00:13:03.722
Who could get through folded arms, man?

00:13:03.722 --> 00:13:05.223
(background noise drowns out speaker)

00:13:05.223 --> 00:13:07.661
So, you know, you're going
to encounter lots of that,

00:13:07.661 --> 00:13:09.553
and sometimes you see it even in Q&amp;A,

00:13:09.553 --> 00:13:11.921
it's in the audience, you let
your eyes go some place else,

00:13:11.921 --> 00:13:15.829
(audio cuts out) like, 'cause
that's who you're playing to.

00:13:15.829 --> 00:13:18.263
And yeah, don't let the...

00:13:18.263 --> 00:13:20.553
I'm telling ya, I do regret
how much time I've wasted.

00:13:20.553 --> 00:13:22.442
Especially now that I know
I'm finishing, I'm like,

00:13:22.442 --> 00:13:24.933
I feel like Schindler at
the end of Schindler's List,

00:13:24.933 --> 00:13:26.851
(background noise drowns out
speaker) saved so many lives.

00:13:26.851 --> 00:13:30.594
Like, if I had just, in order
to do this did this instead,

00:13:30.594 --> 00:13:32.176
concentrated on creating rather

00:13:32.176 --> 00:13:35.047
than whining or bitching
or something like that.

00:13:35.047 --> 00:13:37.926
I'd have so much more
to show for my career.

00:13:37.926 --> 00:13:40.205
But don't let that trap you, man,

00:13:40.205 --> 00:13:44.004
don't let saying throw you off
your game, they're going to say.

00:13:44.004 --> 00:13:45.543
And, you know what, they're right.

00:13:45.543 --> 00:13:46.394
They're absolutely right.

00:13:46.394 --> 00:13:48.204
Everything they say, every
horrible thing they say

00:13:48.204 --> 00:13:50.063
about your work, they're absolutely right.

00:13:50.063 --> 00:13:51.565
They're not lying, that's how they feel.

00:13:51.565 --> 00:13:53.535
But make sure you listen
to the other people

00:13:53.535 --> 00:13:56.053
who say all the good, who say
exactly what you want to know,

00:13:56.053 --> 00:13:57.625
who say exactly what
you're begging to hear,

00:13:57.625 --> 00:14:00.969
what you dreamed about hearing
when you wrote that one line

00:14:00.969 --> 00:14:05.104
years prior, don't forget to
give them the time of day too.

00:14:05.104 --> 00:14:06.463
We spend so much time focusing

00:14:06.463 --> 00:14:08.094
on the bitchers and negative people.

00:14:08.094 --> 00:14:10.172
Give a lot of attention to
the people that like it,

00:14:10.172 --> 00:14:12.574
'cause that's why you're doing
it, that's why you're there.

00:14:12.574 --> 00:14:14.214
I'm not going to do what
everybody else does.

00:14:14.214 --> 00:14:15.944
I'm just going to do what I want to do,

00:14:15.944 --> 00:14:18.563
because life's too short,
and I can't live up

00:14:18.563 --> 00:14:20.013
to other people's expectations,

00:14:20.013 --> 00:14:22.556
I can only do what I can
deliver in this life.

00:14:22.556 --> 00:14:24.207
So I put that on my wrist to remind me

00:14:24.207 --> 00:14:27.005
all the time to trust in self.

00:14:27.005 --> 00:14:29.046
And, you know, I won't up, and, you know,

00:14:29.046 --> 00:14:32.269
(audio cuts out) I made
Cop Out, and Jersey Girl.

00:14:32.269 --> 00:14:35.486
You can things up, but
you can learn from that.

00:14:35.486 --> 00:14:38.725
There's no such thing as absolute failure,

00:14:38.725 --> 00:14:42.476
except sitting there and not trying.

00:14:42.476 --> 00:14:46.023
That's absolute failure, but
even if you fail at something,

00:14:46.023 --> 00:14:47.872
failure is kind of success training,

00:14:47.872 --> 00:14:51.053
'cause you learn how to do
it better the next time.

00:14:51.053 --> 00:14:53.874
As long as you try again, you're smarter

00:14:53.874 --> 00:14:56.443
than the average bear, some
people kind of try something,

00:14:56.443 --> 00:14:58.501
it falls apart, and they walk away.

00:14:58.501 --> 00:15:00.144
There's a bunch of cats who, I get it,

00:15:00.144 --> 00:15:01.743
they look at it and like, "This is stupid.

00:15:01.743 --> 00:15:03.943
"Why would you waste your opportunities,"

00:15:03.943 --> 00:15:05.734
or something, they seem to think that

00:15:05.734 --> 00:15:07.783
this is something given by others.

00:15:07.783 --> 00:15:11.002
They still see it as, you've
been given an opportunity.

00:15:11.002 --> 00:15:12.303
No, nobody gave me jack.

00:15:12.303 --> 00:15:14.534
I went and created an
opportunity for myself.

00:15:14.534 --> 00:15:16.334
Nobody ever shows up at
your door, knocks and says,

00:15:16.334 --> 00:15:17.863
"Hey, man, you want to tell stories?

00:15:17.863 --> 00:15:19.405
"Here's a bunch of
money, go make pretend."

00:15:19.405 --> 00:15:20.815
And that doesn't happen.

00:15:20.815 --> 00:15:24.027
You got to make steps toward
that thing, in the first place.

00:15:24.027 --> 00:15:26.294
I spend a lot of time
trying to explain to people

00:15:26.294 --> 00:15:30.303
the difference between
given an opportunity

00:15:30.303 --> 00:15:33.365
and creating your world,
creating your own opportunity,

00:15:33.365 --> 00:15:34.703
'cause if you create
your own opportunities,

00:15:34.703 --> 00:15:35.655
you're beholden to nobody.

00:15:35.655 --> 00:15:37.556
And then people can't say things like,

00:15:37.556 --> 00:15:38.574
"You shouldn't do this."

00:15:38.574 --> 00:15:39.425
I'm like, "Why?"

00:15:39.425 --> 00:15:40.624
So long as I keep my budgets low,

00:15:40.624 --> 00:15:42.515
make sure everyone gets
their money back and stuff,

00:15:42.515 --> 00:15:43.807
I can do whatever goofy thing I want,

00:15:43.807 --> 00:15:44.776
'cause I'll tell you right now,

00:15:44.776 --> 00:15:46.736
I don't want to go to my
grave one day being like,

00:15:46.736 --> 00:15:49.299
"I should've made the walrus
movies," and then drop dead.

00:15:49.299 --> 00:15:50.349
I want to be the guy that's like,

00:15:50.349 --> 00:15:51.661
"Can't believe we made that walrus movie,"

00:15:51.661 --> 00:15:53.644
and then drop dead, be satisfied.

00:15:53.644 --> 00:15:56.526
'Cause we're I'm talking
about reshaping the world

00:15:56.526 --> 00:15:59.504
or big important issues, man,
this is all micro, not macro.

00:15:59.504 --> 00:16:02.027
It all begins with you.

00:16:02.027 --> 00:16:03.613
That's about belief, you can't expect

00:16:03.613 --> 00:16:05.764
a bunch of people out there
to believe you are this thing

00:16:05.764 --> 00:16:07.674
unless you be this thing yourself.

00:16:07.674 --> 00:16:09.793
We shot this flick, it's called Tusk,

00:16:09.793 --> 00:16:11.134
it stars this guy, Michael Parks,

00:16:11.134 --> 00:16:13.172
who was in this movie Red
State I did three years ago,

00:16:13.172 --> 00:16:15.674
Justin Long is the guy,
he turns into a walrus.

00:16:15.674 --> 00:16:18.625
And I swear to you, now, this
all came from a dopey podcast

00:16:18.625 --> 00:16:20.628
of us going, "Wouldn't it be funny if?"

00:16:20.628 --> 00:16:22.435
It's hands down the best
thing I've ever done.

00:16:22.435 --> 00:16:23.268
- Seriously?

00:16:23.268 --> 00:16:24.101
And the whole...

00:16:24.101 --> 00:16:25.388
I'm not lying, dude, I
know I usually say that

00:16:25.388 --> 00:16:27.510
on every movie that I've
made, but I mean it.

00:16:27.510 --> 00:16:29.592
This movie looks beautiful,
this is the movie

00:16:29.592 --> 00:16:32.602
that the guy who made
Clerks dreamed about making.

00:16:32.602 --> 00:16:34.152
'Cause I didn't want to make Clerks,

00:16:34.152 --> 00:16:35.963
I eventually made Clerks,
but what I wanted to make

00:16:35.963 --> 00:16:37.341
was David Lynch movies.

00:16:37.341 --> 00:16:39.534
I loved how weird and
wonderful David Lynch was,

00:16:39.534 --> 00:16:41.012
but I didn't have enough
talent to do that,

00:16:41.012 --> 00:16:42.563
I had enough talent to make Clerks.

00:16:42.563 --> 00:16:45.053
20 years later, I have enough
talent to go for the things

00:16:45.053 --> 00:16:48.599
that I dreamed about when
I was a nascent beginner.

00:16:48.599 --> 00:16:51.282
And part of the reason is
like, all right, up your game,

00:16:51.282 --> 00:16:53.421
see how far you could push
it, but the other reason

00:16:53.421 --> 00:16:56.553
is I got a lot of people who
listen to me and watch me

00:16:56.553 --> 00:16:58.413
and pay attention to the stuff I do.

00:16:58.413 --> 00:17:00.160
You got a captive audience, man,

00:17:00.160 --> 00:17:02.163
and if you want art in your life,

00:17:02.163 --> 00:17:04.311
you want more people
experience what you've had,

00:17:04.311 --> 00:17:05.713
and all I did was try.

00:17:05.713 --> 00:17:07.158
I had this idea about making a movie

00:17:07.158 --> 00:17:09.652
set in a convenience store,
and I tried it, and it worked.

00:17:09.652 --> 00:17:13.161
And the life's been blowjobs
and donuts ever since, right?

00:17:13.161 --> 00:17:14.821
So you want to encourage
people to do that.

00:17:14.821 --> 00:17:15.654
- From one woman, by the way.

00:17:15.654 --> 00:17:17.666
- Yeah yeah yeah, not
that Christian girlfriend.

00:17:17.666 --> 00:17:18.499
- Yeah.

00:17:18.499 --> 00:17:19.332
(audience laughter)

00:17:19.332 --> 00:17:20.632
- So you want to encourage
others to do that,

00:17:20.632 --> 00:17:22.101
you want others to kind of feel that,

00:17:22.101 --> 00:17:23.712
I'm not saying everyone's
got to be film maker,

00:17:23.712 --> 00:17:25.808
everyone's got to make TV,
everyone's got to write books, but

00:17:25.808 --> 00:17:27.690
why not do a little?

00:17:27.690 --> 00:17:29.299
People get in this mindset of like,

00:17:29.299 --> 00:17:30.788
"We're the audience and
they're the film makers,

00:17:30.788 --> 00:17:32.158
"and we'll watch, and they'll make."

00:17:32.158 --> 00:17:34.088
Anybody can do this
crap, I've spent 20 years

00:17:34.088 --> 00:17:35.319
trying to tell people like,

00:17:35.319 --> 00:17:37.357
"Pick up a camera, it doesn't take much."

00:17:37.357 --> 00:17:40.117
Podcast makes it even
more of a great equalizer,

00:17:40.117 --> 00:17:42.027
'cause that requires a
cell phone, a laptop,

00:17:42.027 --> 00:17:44.279
you sit down with your
friends, your girlfriend,

00:17:44.279 --> 00:17:45.707
your boyfriend, your mother.

00:17:45.707 --> 00:17:47.367
I've sat down with my
mother and podcasted,

00:17:47.367 --> 00:17:48.378
and you just talk.

00:17:48.378 --> 00:17:50.827
Anybody could do that,
that doesn't take talent.

00:17:50.827 --> 00:17:53.498
But on that podcast, there
were so many people on Twitter

00:17:53.498 --> 00:17:56.870
from that episode talking
about, it was really interesting

00:17:56.870 --> 00:17:59.599
to hear you guys building the idea.

00:17:59.599 --> 00:18:01.270
I didn't know that's how it worked.

00:18:01.270 --> 00:18:04.890
And that grabbed me, because
that was me 20 years ago going,

00:18:04.890 --> 00:18:07.978
"How, how do I get from here to there?"

00:18:07.978 --> 00:18:10.130
And here it was, we'd
lay them out a little bit

00:18:10.130 --> 00:18:11.539
on a blueprint on the podcast,

00:18:11.539 --> 00:18:14.140
you heard us creating a story,
you heard me going like,

00:18:14.140 --> 00:18:15.099
"Yeah, you could do this."

00:18:15.099 --> 00:18:16.340
How simple it was, it was just

00:18:16.340 --> 00:18:18.330
demystifying the process a little bit.

00:18:18.330 --> 00:18:21.881
And you do that as a clarion
call to those out there going,

00:18:21.881 --> 00:18:24.232
"Look, hear how simple it is now?"

00:18:24.232 --> 00:18:26.821
We showed you how simple
it was once to make Clerks,

00:18:26.821 --> 00:18:28.702
black and white movie, pick up a camera,

00:18:28.702 --> 00:18:29.900
anybody could do this.

00:18:29.900 --> 00:18:32.101
Now with this episode of this podcast

00:18:32.101 --> 00:18:33.711
that turned into this movie Tusk,

00:18:33.711 --> 00:18:36.031
it's this blueprint for
anybody, for the kid,

00:18:36.031 --> 00:18:38.361
for the 13 year old, for the 45 year old,

00:18:38.361 --> 00:18:39.940
for the 60 year old mother who's like,

00:18:39.940 --> 00:18:41.290
"I always wanted to make a movie."

00:18:41.290 --> 00:18:42.609
To just hear how simple it is,

00:18:42.609 --> 00:18:44.548
sometimes you just need
somebody to be like,

00:18:44.548 --> 00:18:47.067
"It's this simple, dude,"
and give you three steps,

00:18:47.067 --> 00:18:48.491
and suddenly you're like, "That's it?

00:18:48.491 --> 00:18:49.809
"I can take it the rest of the way."

00:18:49.809 --> 00:18:51.808
Getting started is
always the hardest part.

00:18:51.808 --> 00:18:53.730
And you got a bunch of
people listening to you,

00:18:53.730 --> 00:18:55.070
and it's nice that they're the audience

00:18:55.070 --> 00:18:56.621
and I'm glad they're there to support me,

00:18:56.621 --> 00:18:58.788
but I want to give it back,
you want to support them,

00:18:58.788 --> 00:19:01.038
you want to be like, "As happy
as I am, you can do this too.

00:19:01.038 --> 00:19:02.559
"As much as you like watching this,

00:19:02.559 --> 00:19:04.797
"making it's even better, go do it."

00:19:04.797 --> 00:19:07.785
So this Tusk thing has been
more about the audience,

00:19:07.785 --> 00:19:10.140
oddly enough, and not
thinking about my career,

00:19:10.140 --> 00:19:12.642
my standing, where I stand
in the film community,

00:19:12.642 --> 00:19:14.332
whether I've grown as an artist.

00:19:14.332 --> 00:19:16.980
I let go all that stuff, and
all I thought about was like,

00:19:16.980 --> 00:19:19.333
I just want to inspire one
kid to pick up a camera

00:19:19.333 --> 00:19:20.830
and be like, "If that's
how you make a movie,

00:19:20.830 --> 00:19:21.860
"I want to do it too."

00:19:21.860 --> 00:19:24.661
I'm just trying to secure my
future, and watch good movies,

00:19:24.661 --> 00:19:26.923
and the way to do that is
to show a bunch of people

00:19:26.923 --> 00:19:29.001
whose voices are different than anything

00:19:29.001 --> 00:19:31.062
you've ever heard before,
not the same thing

00:19:31.062 --> 00:19:33.868
you see on TV or movies, to
show them how simple it is,

00:19:33.868 --> 00:19:35.990
to show them that their
voice is their currency.

00:19:35.990 --> 00:19:38.139
The thing that you have to
say, that special thing,

00:19:38.139 --> 00:19:41.018
that story you have to
tell, nobody else can tell.

00:19:41.018 --> 00:19:42.590
And you want to encourage
people to do that,

00:19:42.590 --> 00:19:44.042
'cause that's all I did, people go,

00:19:44.042 --> 00:19:45.232
"Oh, you've been very fortunate and

00:19:45.232 --> 00:19:47.583
"what a great career you
had," and the only thing is,

00:19:47.583 --> 00:19:49.832
I one day sat there, watched enough movies

00:19:49.832 --> 00:19:52.289
and TV to be like, "Why not me?

00:19:52.289 --> 00:19:54.251
"They're doing it, what's the difference?"

00:19:54.251 --> 00:19:56.273
And it just took a little bit of Slacker,

00:19:56.273 --> 00:19:59.350
Richard Linklater from
Slacker, demystify the process.

00:19:59.350 --> 00:20:01.827
So for me, with Tusk and this podcast,

00:20:01.827 --> 00:20:03.519
the episode that birthed the movie,

00:20:03.519 --> 00:20:04.941
and the whole process of the movie

00:20:04.941 --> 00:20:07.875
is just a long way of saying,
"Here are your blueprints, go,

00:20:07.875 --> 00:20:10.109
"do what I did, because it rocks."

00:20:10.109 --> 00:20:12.538
How does one spin so many plates at once?

00:20:12.538 --> 00:20:14.738
Well, there's no formula,
and to be honest with you,

00:20:14.738 --> 00:20:16.951
it's a very dangerous pastime,
not as dangerous as, say,

00:20:16.951 --> 00:20:20.028
juggling chainsaws, but
you tend to get swamped

00:20:20.028 --> 00:20:23.040
by your own amount of
projects that you float,

00:20:23.040 --> 00:20:24.769
the amount of boats that you float, man,

00:20:24.769 --> 00:20:26.172
could come back, turn around like

00:20:26.172 --> 00:20:28.111
a Royal Navy and sink your fat ass.

00:20:28.111 --> 00:20:30.691
So basically, you got to feel
what's right for you, man.

00:20:30.691 --> 00:20:32.801
It helps to have a wonderful
support team around ya,

00:20:32.801 --> 00:20:34.923
I have fantastic people in my life,

00:20:34.923 --> 00:20:37.484
not the least of which
is that dog, Shecky!

00:20:37.484 --> 00:20:39.634
Who make everything I do a lot easier.

00:20:39.634 --> 00:20:41.434
I come up with ideas that I want to do,

00:20:41.434 --> 00:20:44.575
and other people actually
make it happen, implement it.

00:20:44.575 --> 00:20:45.862
So that helps to have a team.

00:20:45.862 --> 00:20:47.982
If you don't have a team,
you got to figure out

00:20:47.982 --> 00:20:49.625
what that balance is for yourself.

00:20:49.625 --> 00:20:51.943
You can't take on too
much, but at the same time,

00:20:51.943 --> 00:20:53.903
you don't want to just do one thing.

00:20:53.903 --> 00:20:55.322
I like to spin a lot of plates,

00:20:55.322 --> 00:20:57.903
because you put all
your eggs in one basket,

00:20:57.903 --> 00:21:00.359
don't work out, you're bored,
you've wasted all that time.

00:21:00.359 --> 00:21:02.303
But the fact of the
matter is, back in the day

00:21:02.303 --> 00:21:05.232
we used to make a movie, and
then work on just the movie

00:21:05.232 --> 00:21:06.990
and wait till the movie was out,

00:21:06.990 --> 00:21:09.331
and then figure out what to do next.

00:21:09.331 --> 00:21:11.300
It would lead sometimes to like,

00:21:11.300 --> 00:21:12.772
let's say you made Jersey Girl.

00:21:12.772 --> 00:21:14.371
You make the movie over
the course of a while,

00:21:14.371 --> 00:21:15.730
it comes out, and then phoom,

00:21:15.730 --> 00:21:17.588
that's it, it's over
in a week or two weeks.

00:21:17.588 --> 00:21:19.837
So I like to spin as
many plates as possible.

00:21:19.837 --> 00:21:21.698
That way like, if this
thing isn't panning out,

00:21:21.698 --> 00:21:24.827
or this thing is too
far off in the distance,

00:21:24.827 --> 00:21:26.707
or this ain't going to
happen the way I want it.

00:21:26.707 --> 00:21:28.666
Boom, we could shift
over to something else.

00:21:28.666 --> 00:21:31.446
So that's why I do it, but
when you do, be careful.

00:21:31.446 --> 00:21:33.067
You got to find the right balance,

00:21:33.067 --> 00:21:34.527
I wish there was a formula to give you.

00:21:34.527 --> 00:21:37.287
There's not, it's all up
to you, it's inside of you.

00:21:37.287 --> 00:21:39.376
Just to as much as you can
before you get to the point

00:21:39.376 --> 00:21:40.868
where like, "I can't take it anymore,"

00:21:40.868 --> 00:21:42.377
don't kill yourself for art.

00:21:42.377 --> 00:21:43.907
Although if you're going to
kill yourself for anything,

00:21:43.907 --> 00:21:45.089
art would be the best reason,

00:21:45.089 --> 00:21:46.598
but never kill yourself in this life.

00:21:46.598 --> 00:21:49.538
You're ever going to kill
yourself, try being Batman first.

00:21:49.538 --> 00:21:50.572
And I go into the emergency room,

00:21:50.572 --> 00:21:52.171
and I was like, "Donald Smith?"

00:21:52.171 --> 00:21:53.712
And they're like, "I'm sorry, he passed."

00:21:53.712 --> 00:21:55.343
And they took me into the room.

00:21:55.343 --> 00:21:57.838
There's my father, laying on a gurney.

00:21:57.838 --> 00:22:00.733
Just dead, still has
tubes sticking out of him,

00:22:00.733 --> 00:22:02.953
they were trying to intubate
him or something like that,

00:22:02.953 --> 00:22:06.161
but just motionless, quiet,
no breathing whatsoever.

00:22:06.161 --> 00:22:08.541
And it was weird, to say the least,

00:22:08.541 --> 00:22:10.381
you always know eventually
your parents are going to go,

00:22:10.381 --> 00:22:12.822
but I wasn't ready, I wasn't
ready for that moment.

00:22:12.822 --> 00:22:15.823
And then I walked out,
and I went outside and had

00:22:15.823 --> 00:22:18.030
a cigarette, I was still a
cigarette smoker in those days.

00:22:18.030 --> 00:22:21.551
And I was just thinking
about being half an orphan,

00:22:21.551 --> 00:22:23.863
or halfway to being an orphan,
or something like that.

00:22:23.863 --> 00:22:26.982
My brother comes out, and I was like,

00:22:26.982 --> 00:22:28.762
"Oh, can't believe it, right?"

00:22:28.762 --> 00:22:31.183
He goes, "Yeah, it's pretty tough."

00:22:31.183 --> 00:22:32.602
And I said, "How was it?"

00:22:32.602 --> 00:22:36.021
'Cause my brother was there,
he was staying with my parents.

00:22:36.021 --> 00:22:39.281
And Donald, without missing
a beat, my brother goes,

00:22:39.281 --> 00:22:41.141
"Dad died screaming."

00:22:41.141 --> 00:22:42.433
And I was like, "What?"

00:22:42.433 --> 00:22:44.262
And he goes, "Dad died screaming."

00:22:44.262 --> 00:22:45.333
I asked, "What do you mean?"

00:22:45.333 --> 00:22:46.690
He was like, "Well, he woke up,

00:22:46.690 --> 00:22:48.061
"and he was kicking the sheets off

00:22:48.061 --> 00:22:50.012
"and screaming about being on fire.

00:22:50.012 --> 00:22:51.540
"And he was so hot and so hot,

00:22:51.540 --> 00:22:54.031
"and get him water, and screaming.

00:22:54.031 --> 00:22:55.512
"The screaming got louder and louder,

00:22:55.512 --> 00:22:58.513
"and it reached a fever
pitch, and he died.

00:22:58.513 --> 00:23:00.283
"And I don't know how else to say it,

00:23:00.283 --> 00:23:02.092
"but dad died screaming."

00:23:02.092 --> 00:23:04.222
And that hit me like a Mack truck.

00:23:04.222 --> 00:23:06.823
And I remember thinking,
"Oh my god, in this world,

00:23:06.823 --> 00:23:08.672
"where even a good man like my old man

00:23:08.672 --> 00:23:10.973
"is going to die
screaming, there's no point

00:23:10.973 --> 00:23:14.752
"in not trying to achieve
every dream that I have."

00:23:14.752 --> 00:23:18.022
This is my eventual
end, one day, that's it.

00:23:18.022 --> 00:23:21.033
(applause)

00:23:21.033 --> 00:23:24.783
And I really felt like,
"Man, that's like..."

00:23:26.291 --> 00:23:28.175
This is my future, we're all probably

00:23:28.175 --> 00:23:30.186
going to go out screaming,
so the best thing

00:23:30.186 --> 00:23:33.596
to do prior to that is to
try to pack that life with

00:23:33.596 --> 00:23:38.113
as much wonderfulness, fun,
productivity, surround yourself

00:23:38.113 --> 00:23:40.446
with people that are
going to help you do that.

00:23:40.446 --> 00:23:42.887
'Cause in this world,
there's not a lot of support.

00:23:42.887 --> 00:23:44.997
There's a lot of negativity,
there's a lot of cynicism.

00:23:44.997 --> 00:23:46.486
Even as I sit here and tell that story

00:23:46.486 --> 00:23:48.556
about my father dying, I guarantee you

00:23:48.556 --> 00:23:50.637
there's a cynical head in
this house, maybe more,

00:23:50.637 --> 00:23:53.235
sitting there going, "Who
cares, move on, tell a joke."

00:23:53.235 --> 00:23:55.271
The world is full of why, man.

00:23:55.271 --> 00:23:57.521
You tell people something,
they'll tell you why.

00:23:57.521 --> 00:23:59.091
Throw a rock and you'll
hit somebody with why.

00:23:59.091 --> 00:24:00.391
Like, "Hey man, I want to make a movie."

00:24:00.391 --> 00:24:02.742
"Why, why do you think you could do that?"

00:24:02.742 --> 00:24:05.572
"Why, why, nobody else is doing
it, why are you doing it?"

00:24:05.572 --> 00:24:07.051
There's so much why.

00:24:07.051 --> 00:24:08.750
You go out and you find why not.

00:24:08.750 --> 00:24:10.512
You surround yourself with why not.

00:24:10.512 --> 00:24:12.062
People who are just like, "Why not?"

00:24:12.062 --> 00:24:13.595
Where you're like, "Hey man,
I'm going to try something."

00:24:13.595 --> 00:24:14.938
"Why not, let's give it a shot."

00:24:14.938 --> 00:24:17.487
People who try to help you do your dreams,

00:24:17.487 --> 00:24:20.497
make your dreams come true,
and you do the same for them,

00:24:20.497 --> 00:24:23.269
we're all in this together,
and it costs nothing

00:24:23.269 --> 00:24:25.454
to encourage an artist,
that's why I get up here

00:24:25.454 --> 00:24:28.098
for almost 20 years now
after the movie, man.

00:24:28.098 --> 00:24:29.988
There ain't a lot of film
makers get up after the movie

00:24:29.988 --> 00:24:32.287
and spend more time than
the movie itself talking,

00:24:32.287 --> 00:24:34.687
but I've been trying to
impart one simple lesson

00:24:34.687 --> 00:24:38.016
for nearly 20 years, anybody can do this.

00:24:38.016 --> 00:24:39.081
You know what I'm saying?

00:24:39.081 --> 00:24:41.383
I wasn't born into a business
or the film business,

00:24:41.383 --> 00:24:42.941
or born with a mic in my hands.

00:24:42.941 --> 00:24:45.740
I have no discernible
talent whatsoever, man,

00:24:45.740 --> 00:24:47.571
I have no connections to the business,

00:24:47.571 --> 00:24:49.521
we're from New Jersey and stuff.

00:24:49.521 --> 00:24:51.431
I got lucky, somebody picked
up the movie and boom,

00:24:51.431 --> 00:24:52.691
I was off and running.

00:24:52.691 --> 00:24:55.782
And part of that manifest,
when you're brought into

00:24:55.782 --> 00:24:59.591
the indie film community,
part of your job,

00:24:59.591 --> 00:25:02.034
part of your honor,
part of your privilege,

00:25:02.034 --> 00:25:05.560
is to make sure that keeps
going, to encourage other people.

00:25:05.560 --> 00:25:07.772
To tell people it's worth the shot,

00:25:07.772 --> 00:25:09.189
just like Wayne Gretzky said,

00:25:09.189 --> 00:25:12.178
"You miss 100% of the
shots you never take."

00:25:12.178 --> 00:25:14.602
It's always worth the shot.

00:25:14.602 --> 00:25:15.972
Now, if you're talking about,

00:25:15.972 --> 00:25:17.760
if what your shot is, is you're like,

00:25:17.760 --> 00:25:20.601
"I've always wanted to
kill 12 children, Kevin."

00:25:20.601 --> 00:25:22.264
Don't take that shot at all.

00:25:22.264 --> 00:25:23.396
(audience laughter)

00:25:23.396 --> 00:25:25.038
I'm not talking about that, you know,

00:25:25.038 --> 00:25:28.124
I'm talking about artsy,
you know, writing a blog,

00:25:28.124 --> 00:25:32.462
making some music, making a
movie, making a cupcake store,

00:25:32.462 --> 00:25:36.613
a knitting store, putting
together a YouTube clip,

00:25:36.613 --> 00:25:40.020
just anything that's
not, "Oh, we must do this

00:25:40.020 --> 00:25:42.589
"because it's part of my
job, or this will get money."

00:25:42.589 --> 00:25:45.593
Some you just do just to
see if it could be done.

00:25:45.593 --> 00:25:47.570
Live a why not life, man, because

00:25:47.570 --> 00:25:50.153
we're all going to die
screaming, so make sure,

00:25:50.153 --> 00:25:52.931
when you die screaming,
you're totally fulfilled,

00:25:52.931 --> 00:25:54.769
you're like, "I took
all the shots, I did it,

00:25:54.769 --> 00:25:58.271
"I went after everything I
wanted to, or at least tried."

00:25:58.271 --> 00:26:01.052
Take the shot, the shot
is always worth taking.

00:26:01.052 --> 00:26:04.639
Whether it's a dopey thing like
podcasting or writing a book

00:26:04.639 --> 00:26:08.172
or writing a blog, think about
the encouragement you get,

00:26:08.172 --> 00:26:09.978
when you encourage an artist,
think about the potential

00:26:09.978 --> 00:26:10.949
that comes out of it.

00:26:10.949 --> 00:26:12.160
You tell a dude, "Good job, man."

00:26:12.160 --> 00:26:14.259
That person maybe one day writes that blog

00:26:14.259 --> 00:26:16.339
that you meme out to the entire world,

00:26:16.339 --> 00:26:18.841
'cause it says exactly what
you've always wanted to say,

00:26:18.841 --> 00:26:21.299
but couldn't yourself,
or they write that song

00:26:21.299 --> 00:26:24.707
that you play over and
over after a loved one dies

00:26:24.707 --> 00:26:25.788
'cause it means the world to you,

00:26:25.788 --> 00:26:29.501
or they make that movie
that is the one you cling to

00:26:29.501 --> 00:26:32.152
when everything is going wrong
in life, oh, you pop that in

00:26:32.152 --> 00:26:33.999
and things aren't bad for two hours.

00:26:33.999 --> 00:26:36.120
All that comes from encouraging an artist.

00:26:36.120 --> 00:26:38.379
Nothing good comes from
discouraging an artist.

00:26:38.379 --> 00:26:40.519
So for 20 years I keep
coming up here and going,

00:26:40.519 --> 00:26:43.271
"Go out and doing, why
the aren't you doing this,

00:26:43.271 --> 00:26:46.338
"it's so fun, you should
do it, take your shot,

00:26:46.338 --> 00:26:49.491
"I took my shot and it's
nothing but and donuts."

00:26:49.491 --> 00:26:51.320
(audience laughter)

00:26:51.320 --> 00:26:53.199
So I'm here to tell you,
man, give it a shot.

00:26:53.199 --> 00:26:54.360
Go out and try.

00:26:54.360 --> 00:26:56.610
(applause)

00:26:58.626 --> 00:27:00.149
- Thank you guys so much for watching,

00:27:00.149 --> 00:27:03.548
I made this video because
TheNeckshot asked me to.

00:27:03.548 --> 00:27:04.950
So if there's a famous entrepreneur that

00:27:04.950 --> 00:27:06.979
you want me to profile next, leave it down

00:27:06.979 --> 00:27:08.990
in the comments below and
I'll see what I can do.

00:27:08.990 --> 00:27:11.521
I'd also love to know, what
did you learn today from Kevin,

00:27:11.521 --> 00:27:14.198
what is the lesson that is
most immediately applicable

00:27:14.198 --> 00:27:15.878
to your life or to your business?

00:27:15.878 --> 00:27:17.500
Leave it down in the comments below,

00:27:17.500 --> 00:27:19.157
and I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:27:19.157 --> 00:27:20.567
Finally, I wanted to
give a quick shout out

00:27:20.567 --> 00:27:23.070
to Gourav Junior, thank you
so much for picking up a copy

00:27:23.070 --> 00:27:26.326
of my book, Your One Word,
and making that funny cartoon

00:27:26.326 --> 00:27:29.188
to go alongside it, I
really appreciate it.

00:27:29.188 --> 00:27:30.738
So thank you guys again for watching,

00:27:30.738 --> 00:27:33.934
I believe in you, I hope you
continue to believe in yourself

00:27:33.934 --> 00:27:38.101
and what your one word is,
much love, I'll see you soon.

00:27:44.401 --> 00:27:47.458
- We weren't encouraged to dream, so now,

00:27:47.458 --> 00:27:50.483
I'm a standing testimony
to the possibility

00:27:50.483 --> 00:27:53.733
of normalcy having everything they want

00:27:54.902 --> 00:27:56.382
by simply heading toward it.

00:27:56.382 --> 00:27:58.720
I have no talent, no discernible talent,

00:27:58.720 --> 00:28:02.349
no abilities and stuff like
that, but I've got will.

00:28:02.349 --> 00:28:04.384
I don't have skill, I've been
told that a zillion times

00:28:04.384 --> 00:28:05.436
by the critics over and over,

00:28:05.436 --> 00:28:06.857
I know that I don't have skill.

00:28:06.857 --> 00:28:09.716
But I have will, and will will
take you a lot farther, man.

00:28:09.716 --> 00:28:11.485
Everyone in this room is a unique,

00:28:11.485 --> 00:28:13.635
powerful content generator.

00:28:13.635 --> 00:28:16.956
Out of the million stories to
tell that have not been told,

00:28:16.956 --> 00:28:19.777
and they're different than mine,
and hers, and his, and his.

00:28:19.777 --> 00:28:21.724
Everybody could do this.

00:28:21.724 --> 00:28:25.523
I'm an absolute sham, and I've
done it for 22 years, man.

00:28:25.523 --> 00:28:28.270
Anybody could do this, I'm
not saying be the film maker.

00:28:28.270 --> 00:28:29.878
Just self-express.

00:28:29.878 --> 00:28:32.531
Let 'em know you were here,
tell 'em what's here and here.

00:28:32.531 --> 00:28:34.180
We're all in this together and we all

00:28:34.180 --> 00:28:36.908
have the answers to each
other's questions and problems.

00:28:36.908 --> 00:28:38.719
We just have to relate to one another.

00:28:38.719 --> 00:28:41.584
And that this is the way it
is, is to just self-express,

00:28:41.584 --> 00:28:43.663
whether it's in social media
or something like that,

00:28:43.663 --> 00:28:45.303
which is better from where
you're just saying like,

00:28:45.303 --> 00:28:46.446
"This movie sucks."

00:28:46.446 --> 00:28:48.088
Tell people some that happened to you,

00:28:48.088 --> 00:28:49.363
man, share your story.

00:28:49.363 --> 00:28:51.165
I always advocate podcasting.

00:28:51.165 --> 00:28:55.074
Podcasting is responsible for
the last two movies I made.

00:28:55.074 --> 00:28:57.515
That might scare you away,
don't let it scare you.

00:28:57.515 --> 00:28:59.504
Podcasting is for anybody, sit down,

00:28:59.504 --> 00:29:01.393
you have the equipment,
it's on your phone.

00:29:01.393 --> 00:29:02.673
You sit down, you talk to a friend,

00:29:02.673 --> 00:29:05.595
talk to a loved one, talk
to your (audio cuts out).

00:29:05.595 --> 00:29:08.614
You send it to iTunes, and
boom, you're a podcaster.

00:29:08.614 --> 00:29:11.096
Sit down and record with
people that you love.

00:29:11.096 --> 00:29:12.416
We have this weird world where

00:29:12.416 --> 00:29:14.036
we know what everybody looks like,

00:29:14.036 --> 00:29:15.595
'cause we shove cameras
in each other's faces,

00:29:15.595 --> 00:29:19.234
you will never forget what
anybody looks like when they die.

00:29:19.234 --> 00:29:21.696
But when people die, first thing you lose

00:29:21.696 --> 00:29:23.344
is the sound of their voice, man.

00:29:23.344 --> 00:29:24.955
And we don't run around
with tape recorders

00:29:24.955 --> 00:29:27.889
recording everybody's voices, so use that,

00:29:27.889 --> 00:29:31.112
the decision to self-express,
as a reason to sit down

00:29:31.112 --> 00:29:33.289
and record with people who you love,

00:29:33.289 --> 00:29:36.354
people who may not be here much longer.

00:29:36.354 --> 00:29:37.878
But most importantly, do what you can,

00:29:37.878 --> 00:29:39.838
put yourself out there, let everybody know

00:29:39.838 --> 00:29:42.171
who you are, man, and share.

00:29:43.142 --> 00:29:46.838
I'll get something out of it,
for years, I've loved this.

00:29:46.838 --> 00:29:50.778
I promise you, you self-express
in any way, shape or form,

00:29:50.778 --> 00:29:52.350
and I'm not saying podcast or movie,

00:29:52.350 --> 00:29:54.058
but those are definitely some options,

00:29:54.058 --> 00:29:56.778
you get out there and share
who you are with people.

00:29:56.778 --> 00:29:59.576
This will happen somewhere
over the course of your life,

00:29:59.576 --> 00:30:01.098
this has happened to me multiple times,

00:30:01.098 --> 00:30:03.328
and it's been profoundly
moving every time it does.

00:30:03.328 --> 00:30:05.546
Go out and talk, just talk, just share,

00:30:05.546 --> 00:30:06.910
tell about your life and stuff.

00:30:06.910 --> 00:30:08.840
One day, somebody will
come up to you and say,

00:30:08.840 --> 00:30:11.066
"Something you said saved my life,"

00:30:11.066 --> 00:30:12.558
and they will tell you a long anecdote

00:30:12.558 --> 00:30:14.696
about how some throwaway comment you made

00:30:14.696 --> 00:30:17.228
made all the difference,
and everyone in this room

00:30:17.228 --> 00:30:19.747
can make all the difference
for somebody else, man.

00:30:19.747 --> 00:30:22.098
Get out there and (audio
cuts out), and yes,

00:30:22.098 --> 00:30:24.069
social media is fine for
tagging around things

00:30:24.069 --> 00:30:26.079
and being like, "Oh, take a look at that."

00:30:26.079 --> 00:30:27.130
(audience laughter)

00:30:27.130 --> 00:30:29.950
But it's also good for a
lot more than that, man.

00:30:29.950 --> 00:30:32.901
It's fun to come watch stuff,
it's way more fun to make it.

00:30:32.901 --> 00:30:35.978
I implore you all, go out
and be the content generators

00:30:35.978 --> 00:30:37.519
you are and tell some stories.

00:30:37.519 --> 00:30:38.834
Thanks for being here, everybody.

00:30:38.834 --> 00:30:39.667
(applause)

